stdio fixes from libc-test. scanf and wscanf are rebuilt on musl
1.2.5's vfscanf (MIT, https://musl.libc.org): a field ends at its
width, numbers too (mlibc's took every digit of "%2d"); %c takes exactly
its width or fails; %n$ takes the nth argument; input failure and
matching failure differ; integers come from musl's __intscan
(mlibc/int-scan.hpp, which ports/mlibc/musl keeps) and take C23's 0b
prefix (functional/sscanf, fscanf, fwscanf). Also: fputc and putc
return the byte written as an unsigned char (fputc returned 1); a
memstream seek before the start fails with EINVAL and leaves the
stream where it was; fmemopen in append mode writes at the end of the
contents, and a write that makes them longer ends them with a null
byte where one fits, as POSIX and glibc say (functional/memstream);
ftello on an appending stream with unwritten output counts from the
end of the file (regression/ftello-unflushed-append); a stream on a
descriptor isatty fails on, a closed one, is not a terminal, and a
read or write that fails for any reason sets the error indicator
(regression/rewind-clear-error); fread returns what it read before an
error; vasprintf fails with ENOMEM when memory runs out instead of
aborting (regression/setenv-oom); wprintf's %s counts wide characters
for its precision and width; swprintf leaves a string even when the
output did not fit (functional/swprintf). Meant for upstream, the
scanf part as a proposal.

